6.Plaintiff moved to Colonial City in July of 2021, and in August she commenced her work as a member of defendant’s faculty. In July 2021 she took the Madison State Bar Examination, and was notified in November 2021 that she had passed that exam.

7.On March 15, 2022, plaintiff was told by Dean Benedict that her appointment would be terminated as of the end of the current academic year. Plaintiff completed her teaching and other responsibilities over the remainder of the spring semester and was paid her salary through the end of July 2022.

8.At the time plaintiff was discharged, she asked defendant for a statement of the reasons for her discharge, and was informed that defendant’s budgetary problems made it necessary to reduce expenses. Plaintiff did at all times between August 1, 2021, and July 31, 2022, satisfactorily perform

9.After her discharge by defendant, plaintiff was unable to find employment as a law professor for the academic year 2022-2023. On February 1, 2023, she accepted a job as an associate attorney with the Colonial City law firm of Dewey, Wyndham & Howe, at an annual salary of $60,000, where she continues to be employed at the present time.

Plaintiff’s complaint goes on to ask for damages in the amount of lost wages at the rate of $8,000 per month from August 1, 2022 through January 31, 2023, and at the rate of $3,000 per month for the 18-month period thereafter, plus interest.

